--- title The templates under system/standard
--- about A script builds its own values from these: an error, a script
--- about record, a port spec, a font. boot/sysobj.reb declares
--- about twenty-nine of them and the C compiles that into the boot
--- about block, so their existence is the interpreter's own work.
--- about
--- about What is here is the REBOL half: the templates a borrowed
--- about REBOL file fills after the declaration, and what a script
--- about does with any of them. The Java side asserts the shapes,
--- about because that is the side that has to build them.
--- about
--- about Written after seventeen of the twenty-nine turned out to be
--- about missing with nothing testing any of it. Rebol's own suite
--- about never asks the SYSTEM object anything.
--- about
--- about Every result below was run on a real 3.22.1 before being
--- about written down. That caught four wrong expectations out of
--- about twenty-two, all of them a guess about which layer fills a
--- about slot: font and para are none without VID, and bincode and
--- about utype are objects where sysobj.reb declares none.

--- id system-standard/a-template-is-an-object
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb, the standard object
--- code
object? system/standard/script
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/make-over-a-template-answers-an-object
--- origin sys-base.reb line 132, which is how DO builds system/script
--- code
object? make system/standard/script [title: "a title"]
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/make-over-a-template-keeps-its-words
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb, script: construct [title header parent path args]
--- code
mold words-of make system/standard/script [title: "a title"]
--- result
"[title header parent path args]"

--- id system-standard/a-field-set-through-make-holds-what-was-given
--- origin sys-base.reb line 132
--- code
s: make system/standard/script [title: "a title"]
s/title
--- result
"a title"

--- id system-standard/a-field-not-set-stays-none
--- note A template is a record of empty slots, so what MAKE leaves
--- note alone reads as none rather than raising.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb
--- code
s: make system/standard/script [title: "a title"]
none? s/args
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/make-does-not-change-the-template
--- note The one property that makes a template reusable. A MAKE that
--- note wrote through would give the next caller the last caller's
--- note values.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb
--- code
make system/standard/script [title: "changed"]
none? system/standard/script/title
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/do-builds-its-script-record-this-way
--- note The whole line from sys-base.reb, which is Rebol's own DO.
--- origin sys-base.reb lines 132 to 138
--- code
s: make system/standard/script compose [
    title: "a title"
    header: none
    parent: none
    path: none
    args: none
]
mold words-of s
--- result
"[title header parent path args]"

--- id system-standard/the-error-template-names-what-an-error-carries
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb, error: construct [code type id arg1 arg2 arg3 near where]
--- code
mold words-of system/standard/error
--- result
"[code type id arg1 arg2 arg3 near where]"

--- id system-standard/a-raised-error-carries-the-templates-fields
--- note The template is only worth having if a real error matches it.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb and boot/errors.reb together
--- code
e: try [1 / 0]
all [
    true? find words-of system/standard/error 'code
    true? find words-of system/standard/error 'id
    true? find words-of system/standard/error 'near
    e/id = 'zero-divide
]
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/a-port-spec-derives-from-the-head
--- note port-spec-file is `make port-spec-head [path]`, so it carries
--- note the head's three words before its own one.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb
--- code
mold words-of system/standard/port-spec-file
--- result
"[title scheme ref path]"

--- id system-standard/a-derived-spec-is-its-own-object
--- note MAKE over a prototype copies rather than aliases, or writing
--- note one port's spec would write every port's.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb
--- code
same? system/standard/port-spec-head system/standard/port-spec-file
--- result
#(false)

--- id system-standard/enum-is-filled-by-a-borrowed-rebol-file
--- note sysobj.reb declares `enum: none` and mezz-func.reb line 110
--- note replaces it with an object. This is the layer rule working:
--- note the interpreter declares the slot and REBOL fills it.
--- origin mezz-func.reb line 110
--- code
object? system/standard/enum
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/enum-carries-what-mezz-func-gives-it
--- origin mezz-func.reb line 110
--- code
mold words-of system/standard/enum
--- result
"[title* assert name]"

--- id system-standard/enum-is-what-the-enum-function-builds-from
--- note Which is why the template is there rather than the object
--- note being built inline: ENUM makes one per call.
--- origin mezz-func.reb line 105
--- code
object? make system/standard/enum [name: 'colours]
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/font-is-none-without-vid
--- note Six entries here asserted that font and para are objects,
--- note filled by view-funcs.reb. The binary says otherwise: that
--- note file is in vid-files in make/pre-make.r3 and is included only
--- note when include-vid is set, so a stock 3.22.1 answers none and
--- note has no VIEW at all. JEBOL loads the file unconditionally,
--- note which is a fork to settle rather than a result to assert.
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1 (Rebol/Bulk, 2026-05-27)
--- code
none? system/standard/font
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/para-is-none-without-vid
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1 (Rebol/Bulk, 2026-05-27)
--- code
none? system/standard/para
--- result
#(true)

--- id system-standard/bincode-is-an-object-the-c-fills
--- note Declared `bincode: none` in sysobj.reb and replaced by the
--- note base-code block at u-bincode.c line 178. So a stock build
--- note answers an object, and this one is the C's rather than a
--- note REBOL file's.
--- origin confirmed against R3 3.22.1 (Rebol/Bulk, 2026-05-27)
--- code
mold words-of system/standard/bincode
--- result
"[type buffer buffer-write r-mask w-mask]"

--- id system-standard/every-template-is-reachable-by-path
--- note The list from boot/sysobj.reb, in its own order. A missing
--- note one raises invalid-path rather than answering none, so this
--- note names the first gap.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb, the standard object
--- code
found: copy []
foreach name [
    codec enum error script header scheme port
    port-spec-head port-spec-file port-spec-net port-spec-checksum
    port-spec-crypt port-spec-midi port-spec-serial port-spec-audio
    file-info net-info console-info vector-info date-info handle-info
    midi-info extension stats type-spec bincode utype font para
] [
    unless find words-of system/standard name [append found name]
]
mold found
--- result
"[]"

--- id system-standard/there-are-twenty-nine-of-them
--- note So a field invented here shows up as well as one missing.
--- origin boot/sysobj.reb, the standard object
--- code
length? words-of system/standard
--- result
29
